    56	
    57	**G01 — The client rule permits slashable emissions.**  
    58	Task 8 says that `hd.h_j > hd.h_F` is sufficient to emit a finality pair. The spec also requires a compatible recorded target, no recorded timeout, and a compatible lock. These checks remain necessary when NJ is removed. For example, after the client records a timeout at height `m`, a head can expose a justification at `m`. The plan’s stated rule emits its finality pair and thus creates E1 evidence against the earlier timeout. Task 8’s instruction to port DC conflicts with its explicit rule. Restore the three guards and add rejection tests for each. Port the local record invariant and preservation proofs as well. [Spec:1180](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/spec/fgsa-protocol.tex:1180), [DC client:160](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/decoupled-consensus/verified-consensus/DecoupledConsensusModel/Protocol/ValidatorClient.lean:160).
    59	
    60	**G02 — `twoChanges_Fmove` has a counterexample with valid entries and unique roots.**  
    61	Use four unit-weight validators, quorum three, and `timeoutDelay = 1`. Construct this chain:
    62	
    63	- `B1`, slot 1: three targets for `(1, genesis)`. Its state height is 2.
    64	- `B2`, slot 2: three targets for `(2, B1)`. Its state height is 3, with justified pair `(2, B1)`.
    65	- `B3`, slot 3: three timeouts for `(3, B2)`, each with finality pair `(2, B1)`. Its state height is 4 and it finalizes `B1`.
    66	
    67	Take `Vw = {genesis, B1, B2, B3}`, `m = 4`, `F = genesis`, and `F′ = B1`. All hypotheses of `twoChanges_Fmove` hold; `hP` is vacuous because there is no justification at height 3. Both frames are gap frames with `lo = 3`. However, the old `D` contains genesis and the new `D` does not. An in-memory evaluation of the stated transition confirms these values.
    68	
    69	The proof reverses ancestry: `B ⪯ W` and `F′ ⪯ W` do not imply `F′ ⪯ B`. Preserve the band witnesses, or state equality after cutting the old domain at `F′`. Also split finalization *below* `m−1` from finalization *at* `m−1`: the strict inequality in `finalLock` does not establish `hW` at equality. [Plan:1101](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/plans/2026-09-28-fg-module.md:1101).
    70	
    71	**G03 — The proposed `pickPair` port has the wrong tie behavior.**  
    72	DC’s `pickUnique?` returns `none` when two distinct candidates satisfy the predicate. It does not choose one of them. The plan permits distinct blocks with the same greatest key and says that `pickPair` returns one. With the prescribed DC selector, two distinct height-2 entries with the same root cause fallback to `(0, genesis)`. This falsifies `fBasis_lexMax`; adding the second candidate can also falsify `lexMax_mono`. Neither theorem requires root injectivity. Specify a computable selector with the promised behavior, or make collision freedom part of the view interface and theorem hypotheses. [Plan:570](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/plans/2026-09-28-fg-module.md:570), [DC selector:295](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/decoupled-consensus/verified-consensus/DecoupledConsensusModel/Objects/Blocks.lean:295).
    73	
    74	**G04 — The store boundary does not preserve FG admission.**  
    75	The spec separates the raw tree from the FG-admitted tree. It inserts a block into the latter and calls `update_finality` only if the current `F ⪯ B`. The plan calls its tree “processed” and inserts every block passed to `on_block`; its stated precondition concerns only the parent. An off-F branch can therefore raise `h_max`, enter duty views, and change the frame. Ordinary block validity does not supply the missing FG guard. Either enforce the guard or expose an explicitly named admission operation whose contract requires it. [Spec:338](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/spec/fgsa-protocol.tex:338), [Spec:694](/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/spec/fgsa-protocol.tex:694), [Plan:655](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/plans/2026-09-28-fg-module.md:655).
    76	
    77	**G05 — The F-basis lemma assumes the property that the store must establish.**  
    78	Changing `st.fBasis` to the empty set leaves every field of `CacheInv` unchanged. Thus `CacheInv` cannot prove that a duty view closed under `fBasis` contains either required carrier. `fBasis_lexMax` only assumes that a suitable `K` exists. Add an invariant that records `fBasis ⊆ T`, an accepting carrier that finalizes the current F, and a saved J carrier whose J extends F. Prove initialization and preservation. These facts must supply the duty-view premises.
    79	
    80	The added `hVw` and `hHF` in `fjFrame_D_nonempty` are correct and sufficient; they are not errors. The missing work is to derive them from a retained F carrier. For the later SA walk, also expose the pure geometry facts that put Z in D and give a viable child below `lo`, under ancestor closure. [Plan:681](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/plans/2026-09-28-fg-module.md:681), [Review:52](/Users/francesco/ethereum/lean/available-chain/docs/audit/fg-plan-async-review.md:52).
    81	
    82	**G06 — `provenance_finality` proves a different fact.**  
    83	The registry concerns an emitted finality row and an arbitrary finality QC. The proposed theorem concerns a checkpoint already finalized by `derive D`. A QC need not yet be assembled into such a chain. Moreover, `HeadFields` permits arbitrary values: even with the correct record guards, the initial record can sign fields that have no known justification carrier. This interface is acceptable only with an explicit caller obligation and a theorem for `hd = HeadFields.ofBlock … Hd`.
    84	
