   657	- [ ] **Step 2: Write `Frame.lean`.**
   658	
   659	```lean
   660	abbrev View (V : Type) := Finset (FGBlock V)
   661	
   662	variable {V : Type} [DecidableEq V] [Fintype V] (E : Electorate V) (cfg : FGConfig)
   663	
   664	def hOf (W : FGBlock V) : Height := (derive E cfg W).h
   665	
   666	/-- `H = max({1} ∪ {h(W) : W ∈ V})`. -/
   667	def frontier (Vw : View V) : Height := Vw.sup (hOf E cfg) ⊔ 1
   668	
   669	/-- Collision freedom on a view: roots identify the blocks of the view and their
   670	ancestors (the collision-free hash idealization; DC `RootInjectiveOnAncestors`).
   671	A hypothesis of every lemma that reads the selected pair; no definition assumes
   672	it. -/
   673	def ViewRootInj (Vw : View V) : Prop :=
   674	  ∀ X Y, (∃ W ∈ Vw, FGBlock.Preceq X W) → (∃ W ∈ Vw, FGBlock.Preceq Y W) →
   675	    X.root = Y.root → X = Y
   676	
   677	/-- The lex key `(h_j, J.root)`. -/
   678	def lexKey (p : Height × FGBlock V) : Lex (ℕ × ℕ) := toLex (p.1, p.2.root.value)
   679	
   680	structure Frame (V : Type) where
   681	  F : FGBlock V
   682	  H : Height
   683	  j : Height
   684	  J : FGBlock V
   685	  Z : FGBlock V
   686	  lo : Height
   687	  D : Finset (FGBlock V)
   688	  contest : Bool
   689	```
   690	
   691	  `lexMax E cfg F Vw` returns the lex-greatest `(h_j, J)` over `W ∈ Vw` with `F ⪯ (derive W).J`, or `(0, genesis)` if there is none:
   692	  - Compute the greatest key with `Finset.sup` over `Lex (ℕ × ℕ)`.
   693	  - Take the pair with that key through `pickPair`, a port of DC's `pickUnique?` (`DC/DecoupledConsensusModel/Objects/Blocks.lean:295–301`), on the Finset of candidate PAIRS (the image of the view, so that two carriers of one pair count once), with `(0, genesis)` as the default. Make `pickPair` generic in the element type and the key, so that Task 7's `witness` can use it on blocks.
   694	  - `pickPair` has DC's tie behavior: it returns the unique candidate with the greatest key, and `none` when two DISTINCT candidates have that key (a root collision). Then `lexMax` falls back to `(0, genesis)`. Do not choose one of them: there is no computable order on `FGBlock` that the spec names.
   695	  - Under `ViewRootInj Vw`, two candidates with one key are one pair (same `h_j`, same root, so the same J), so `pickPair` returns it. Every lemma that reads the selected pair (`fBasis_lexMax`, `lexMax_mono`, `fjFrame_D_nonempty`, the Task 12 and 13 lemmas) has `ViewRootInj` as a hypothesis.
   696	  - Keep `lexMax` computable, so that the `decide` examples reduce.
   697	
   698	  The fallback `(0, genesis)`: if `F ≠ genesis` and no pair in the view extends F, then `J = genesis`, and `F ⪯ J` fails. The spec keeps the F basis in every duty view (spec lines 361–365 and 494), so this case does not occur in a spec duty. Task 7's `fBasis_lexMax` (pinned to `fg.frameCompat.v1`, part (ii)) proves that the fallback is not used when the view holds a block whose J extends F and `ViewRootInj` holds. Keep the fallback; do not change it to `(h_F, F)`, because the spec does not say so.
   699	
   700	```lean
   701	/-- [pin fj.frame.v1] The FJ frame (r148 §2; spec `fg_frame`): `F ⪯ J`; a
   702	contest at `H = j+1` when a height-`H` block does not extend `J`; in a contest
   703	the root is `F` and the band is `{H}`. Frame only: head selection and eligibility are AP data. -/
   704	def fjFrame (F : FGBlock V) (Vw : View V) : Frame V :=
   705	  let H := frontier E cfg Vw
   706	  let p := lexMax E cfg F Vw
   707	  let contest := decide (H = p.1 + 1) &&
   708	    decide (∃ W ∈ Vw, hOf E cfg W = H ∧ ¬ FGBlock.Preceq p.2 W)
   709	  let Z := if H = p.1 + 1 ∧ contest = false then p.2 else F
   710	  let lo := if contest then H else H - 1
   711	  let D := Vw.filter (fun B => FGBlock.preceq F B && FGBlock.preceq Z B &&
   712	    decide (∃ W ∈ Vw, FGBlock.Preceq B W ∧ lo ≤ hOf E cfg W))
   713	  ⟨F, H, p.1, p.2, Z, lo, D, contest⟩
   714	```
