   966	- [ ] **Step 4: Prove the theorems.**
   967	  - `storeInv_on_block`:
   968	    - `closed`, `sub`: `hp`, and `TF` grows only with `T`.
   969	    - `admit`: a new block with `F ⪯ B` enters `TF`. F only moves to a descendant (`update_finality_F_mono`), so `F' ⪯ X` implies `F ⪯ X`, and `X ∈ TF` by the old invariant.
   970	    - `closedF`: take an admitted new `B ∉ st.T`. `F ∈ TF ⊆ T`, so `F ≠ B`. Then `F ⪯ B` gives `F ⪯ B.parent`, and `B.parent ∈ TF` by `admit` and `hp`. If `B ∈ st.T` already, `TF` does not change.
   971	    - `Fmem`: F moves only to `σ.F ∈ acceptViable ⊆ TF`.
   972	    - `hmax`: `frontier (insert B TF) = max (frontier TF) (h B)`. A block that is not admitted does not change `TF` or `h_max` (G04).
   973	    - `pair`: a new pair enters only if `F ⪯ σ.J` and it is lex-greater; otherwise the old pair stays the lex-max over the larger tree. When F advances to `σ.F ⪯ J`, the cached pair still has `F ⪯ J`. Every other pair with `new F ⪯ J'` also had `old F ⪯ J'`, because `old F ⪯ new F`. So the lex-max does not change. `hinj` makes the selected pair unique.
   974	    - `basis`, `bF`, `bW`: at an acceptance, `fBasis := {B, jCarrier, W}`: `(derive B).F = σ.F` (bF), `σ.F ⪯ W` (bW; the filter holds `σ.F` itself, since `σ.F ∈ TF`), and all three are in `TF`. Otherwise `fBasis` and `F` do not change, and `TF` grows. `derive` is a function of the block, so a saved carrier keeps its F.
   975	  - `fBasis_duty`: from `bF`, `bW` and `hVw`. The second part follows from the first by `derive_F_preceq_J` (Task 4).
   976	  - `update_finality_F_mono`: the F branch requires `st.F ⪯ σ.F`; otherwise F is unchanged.
   977	  - `fBasis_lexMax`: `lexMax` is the lex-max over the pairs with `F ⪯ J`, and `K`'s pair is one of them. With `hinj`, the pairs with the greatest key are one pair, so `pickPair` returns it (Task 6).
   978	  - `fjFrame_lo`, `fjFrame_D_viable`: unfold `fjFrame`; `simp` with `Finset.mem_filter`.
   979	  - `fjFrame_D_reachesLo`: take `B ∈ D` and its witness `W` (`B ⪯ W`, `h(W) ≥ lo`). Then `W ∈ D`: `F ⪯ B ⪯ W`, `Z ⪯ B ⪯ W`, and `W` is its own witness.
   980	  - `fjFrame_D_nonempty`: `finalized_below_height` (Task 4) gives `h_F(K) < h(K) ≤ H`. So the view is not empty (`K ∈ Vw`), and a frontier block `W_H ∈ Vw` with `h(W_H) = H` exists (`frontier` is a `sup`; `one_le_h`). `H > h_F(K)`, so `F ⪯ W_H` by `hext`, and `h(W_H) = H ≥ lo`. In the gap and contested cases `Z = F`, so `W_H ∈ D`. In the uncontested case `Z = J`: every height-H block of the view extends J (definition of "uncontested"), so `J ⪯ W_H`; and `F ⪯ J` by `fBasis_lexMax` with `K` (`F = (derive K).F ⪯ (derive K).J`). So `W_H ∈ D`. Do NOT use a witness of the F basis here: it had the greatest height at acceptance time, and a later `lo = H−1` can be above it.
   981	  - `fjFrame_Z_mem_D`: take `B ∈ D`; `Z ⪯ B`, so `Z ∈ Vw` by `hclosed`; `F ⪯ Z` (`hFZ`), `Z ⪯ Z`, and B's witness is a witness of Z.
