  1517	  - `evidence_mono`: the witnesses stay in the larger set.
  1518	  - `effFrame_of_bits`: a carrier K has `h_j(K) < h(K) ≤ m` (`justified_below_height`), so no pair is above `m−1`. If not `E_J`, no pair is at `m−1` (by `huniq`, a pair at `m−1` names `Jm`, which is `E_J`), so `j < m−1`, `H ≠ j+1`: gap, `lo = m−1`. With `m ≥ 2`, the fallback `(0, genesis)` is also below `m−1`. If `E_J`: `F ⪯ Jm` (`hFJ`), so `(m−1, Jm)` is a candidate and the greatest key; with `hinj` the selector returns it, so `j = m−1`, `J = Jm`, `H = j+1`. The contest test is exactly `E_C`.
  1519	  - `twoChanges`: by `effFrame_of_bits`, the phase at k is a function of the two bits at k, and the normalized frame is a function of the phase. `hJ` and `hC` make the bits monotone in k. The rank of `(bit_J, bit_C)` under the table is monotone in both bits.
  1520	  - `twoChanges_count`: the normalized frame changes only where the rank changes (`twoChanges`, second part). The rank is monotone with values in `{0, 1, 2}`, so it rises at most twice.
  1521	  - `twoChanges_nested`: `evidence_mono` gives `hJ` and `hC`; then `twoChanges` with `Fs := fun _ => F`.
  1522	  - `Fmove_below`:
  1523	    1. A pair at `m−1` has J with `h(J) = m−1` (`entry_height`) and a carrier `K ∈ Vw` with `h(K) ≥ m > h(F′)`, so `F′ ⪯ K` (`hext`). `J ⪯ K` (`derive_anchors_preceq`), so J and F′ are comparable (`preceq_total_of_common`). `J ⪯ F′` with `J ≠ F′` would give `h(J) ≤ h(F′) < m−1` (`h_mono`). So `F′ ⪯ J`. Thus the pairs at `m−1` are candidates for F′ exactly when they are candidates for F (`F ⪯ F′`).
  1524	    2. So `j = m−1` under F if and only if under F′, with the same `(j, J)` (`hinj`): the phase and the contest bit agree. In the gap phase the lex-max pair below `m−1` can change; the gap frame does not read it.
  1525	    3. lo agrees (it depends on H and the contest bit). Z: uncontested, `Z = J` in both; otherwise `Z = F` and `Z = F′`.
  1526	    4. D: `F ⪯ F′` makes the F′ filter the F filter plus `F′ ⪯ B`. A removed block B has a witness W with `h(W) ≥ lo ≥ m−1 > h(F′)`, so `F′ ⪯ W` (`hext`); `B ⪯ W`; so B and F′ are comparable, and not `F′ ⪯ B`, so `B ⪯ F′`.
  1527	  - `Fmove_atTop`: `h_F(K) = m−1 ≤ h_j(K) < h(K) ≤ m` gives `h_j(K) = m−1`, so `h_F(K) = h_j(K)` and `J(K) = F(K) = F′` (`F_eq_J_of_heights`). So `(m−1, F′)` is a pair in the view; with `huniq` it is the only pair at `m−1`, and it is a candidate for F and for F′. So `j = m−1`, `J = F′`, `H = j+1` in both frames. Every height-m block extends F′ (`hext`), so there is no contest: uncontested, `Z = F′`, `lo = m−1`. D: both filters are `F′ ⪯ B` (`F ⪯ F′`).
  1528	  - `Fmove_atFrontier`: `finalized_below_height` gives `h(K) > h_F(K) ≥ m`, and `h(K) ≤ frontier`.
  1529	- [ ] **Step 4: Run it; it passes.** Run: `lake build --wfail AvailableChainKitFG`. Expected: PASS. If a statement cannot be proved in this form, do not weaken it silently: record the clause as not covered in Task 14, keep the lemma out of the pin table, and report it.
  1530	- [ ] **Step 5: CHECKPOINT and commit.**
